WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.010
John Cena's Mandarin chinese is
an example of people who learn

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.820
a language for a certain purpose
and pull it off successfully.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:15.000
Hi, Steve Kaufmann here, um, today,
a bit of an unusual subject, maybe,

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:21.320
uh, someone asked me how well does
John Cena speak Mandarin Chinese.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.860
So the title is John Cena speaking
Mandarin Chinese, but I want to

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:30.670
really talk about the importance of
communicating when we learn a language

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:38.040
and then the relative lack of importance
of perfect pronunciation or even other

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:43.365
imperfections in our use of a language
that we learn in order to communicate.

00:00:43.905 --> 00:00:47.805
So someone asked me, and by the way, if
you enjoy these videos, please subscribe,

00:00:47.835 --> 00:00:49.545
click on the bell for notification.

00:00:50.055 --> 00:00:53.205
And if you're following me
on a podcast service, please

00:00:53.235 --> 00:00:55.335
leave a comment or a review.

00:00:56.265 --> 00:01:00.555
So a friend said, what do you
think of John Cena's Chinese?

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.600
To be honest, I don't, didn't know
who John Cena was CENA turns out

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.610
he's some kind of a pro wrestler.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:14.580
Uh, I'm not a big fan of professional
wrestling, uh, but, uh, whatever

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.770
he is a well-known personality.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:17.700
So I looked him up.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.440
So what do I think?

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:24.720
His Chinese is not what
you would call phenomenal.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:29.120
His pronunciation is not very Chinese.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.995
However, It's very pleasing.

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:33.915
He communicates.

00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:37.335
You have the feeling that he
wants to communicate, that he

00:01:37.335 --> 00:01:38.985
likes the Chinese language.

00:01:39.075 --> 00:01:41.685
He likes communicating
with his Chinese fans.

00:01:42.375 --> 00:01:44.505
Uh, there's something very positive.

00:01:44.505 --> 00:01:49.335
Now, I'm not a native speaker of
Chinese so I can't, I can't tell

00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:50.955
you how a Chinese person reacts.

00:01:50.955 --> 00:01:54.645
Maybe some of you who are Chinese can
tell me your reaction to his Chinese.

00:01:54.645 --> 00:01:57.225
But to me, it comes
across as quite positive.

00:01:57.914 --> 00:02:02.744
Um, And, uh, so I, I thought of
all these people that I know who

00:02:02.804 --> 00:02:06.145
communicate very effectively in a
language that they speak imperfectly.

00:02:06.824 --> 00:02:09.845
Uh, we can start with, uh, former
Canadian prime minister, Jean

00:02:09.845 --> 00:02:14.535
Chrétien, and he speaks with a
pronounced the French Canadian accent.

00:02:14.925 --> 00:02:19.545
And even his usage is not always
a hundred percent yet he was very

00:02:19.545 --> 00:02:23.625
effective, so effective communicating
in English that he was prime minister

00:02:23.625 --> 00:02:26.175
of Canada for I think 10 years.

00:02:26.385 --> 00:02:27.495
And was very popular.

00:02:28.455 --> 00:02:34.035
Uh, you know, I it's possible just
as you know, John Cena uses his

00:02:34.035 --> 00:02:39.825
Chinese it's part of his shtick of
connecting with his Chinese fans.

00:02:39.885 --> 00:02:44.085
I think to some extent, or some people
suspected that Jean Chrétien affected

00:02:44.085 --> 00:02:48.495
his, you know, Quebecois accent on his
English, because it was part of his

00:02:48.495 --> 00:02:54.525
persona and he was a very successful uh,
politician, but they both communicate.

00:02:55.005 --> 00:02:59.925
Um, if you watch, uh, Mark Zuckerberg
speak Chinese, my impression

00:02:59.925 --> 00:03:04.345
is that he may in fact speak
Chinese better than John Cena.

00:03:04.365 --> 00:03:04.755
I don't know.

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:11.550
But, uh, he's not tremendous either,
but you have the impression that

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.330
he isn't really communicating
so much as he's performing.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:19.920
Uh, his attitude is, uh,
speaking to his Chinese audience

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.320
in Beijing "oh, look at me.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:23.730
I can speak Chinese.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.170
Uh, I'm very clever.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:25.680
Aren't I?"

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:27.180
Kind of thing.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.690
Uh, so that the audience is kind
of a prop, so at least that's the

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.130
impression that's created with me.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.360
It may not be fair, but you know,
I think the important thing in

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.670
communicating is that genuine
desire to communicate, to connect.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:49.770
And, uh, as I've said, many times,
I, you know, I remember I had a Swiss

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:55.440
banker who spoke English so well, but
had a pronounced Swiss accent, uh, all

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.310
the different people that I've, you
know, done business with who spoke, but

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:04.260
who communicated and they didn't for a
minute, worry about their pronunciation

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.690
or the odd little error in their language.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.700
And so that's where, you know, many of
us, as we're learning a language, of

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.860
course, we want to continue to improve,
but whatever our level, whatever your

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.200
level, whatever you are, you have
to measure what you have achieved

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:23.640
against what your objectives are.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:29.460
So if your objective let's say in
learning Japanese is to absolutely

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:34.950
master the pronunciation to sound
like someone on national radio in

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.489
Japan, and you're going to work on
your pitch accent and that's fine.

00:04:38.489 --> 00:04:39.780
I'm not critical of that.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:43.919
But to suggest that everyone
learning Japanese should put the

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:49.539
same amount of effort into mastering
pitch accent is to my mind silly.

00:04:49.799 --> 00:04:55.110
I have never, I was blithely for all the
time I lived in Japan I never even knew

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.450
such a thing as pitch accent existed.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.990
I'm sure that in trying to imitate
Japanese as spoken by native speakers

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.230
and of course falling short, but
nevertheless, using that as a model

00:05:08.025 --> 00:05:11.815
I would not necessarily imitate
some aspects of pitch accent.

00:05:11.835 --> 00:05:17.085
And similarly in speaking Persian,
which I'm told is a language that has

00:05:17.085 --> 00:05:22.305
pitch accent, if I tried to imitate
the way the native speakers speak, to

00:05:22.305 --> 00:05:26.655
some extent, I will imitate that pitch
accent, but I'm not going to study

00:05:26.655 --> 00:05:29.055
it with the intention of aceing it.

00:05:29.565 --> 00:05:32.815
Uh, it's just part of my
acquisition of the language.

00:05:34.034 --> 00:05:38.385
And, uh, there will be imperfections,
uh, as I've said before there, I don't

00:05:38.385 --> 00:05:43.305
know what we have a Russian person that
works with us and, uh, his, his articles

00:05:43.305 --> 00:05:45.854
use of the article is very haphazard.

00:05:46.094 --> 00:05:50.474
It doesn't prevent him from being a very
comfortable communicator in English.

00:05:50.835 --> 00:05:54.765
Um, you know, people make
mistakes in gender in French.

00:05:55.125 --> 00:05:58.094
I did my video, uh, about
the difficulties of French.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:02.490
Uh, it's, it's important to be
aware of some of these difficulties

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.110
and work on some of them.

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:07.200
I mean, we can work on,
on getting the 'r' right.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.010
But if we can't quite do the guttural
'r' in French a hundred percent like the

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.050
French, it doesn't prevent communication.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:17.715
Uh, I think it's worth working
on the 'eu' sound, the famous...

00:06:17.955 --> 00:06:22.575
go 'ooo' and then  try to say 'e'
and you'll get 'eu' it's worth

00:06:22.605 --> 00:06:26.265
working on it, but if you don't get
it, it's not the end of the world.

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:29.085
Uh, similarly with the,
uh, the nasal sounds.

00:06:29.085 --> 00:06:34.665
I think they are worth working on,
noticing them, trying to do as well as

00:06:34.665 --> 00:06:39.975
you can with the nasal sounds because
it will also help your comprehension,

00:06:40.335 --> 00:06:44.595
but when you speak and when you are
genuinely communicating, communicating

00:06:44.595 --> 00:06:47.235
with someone, just communicate.

00:06:47.685 --> 00:06:52.665
So if I get back to John Cena and his
Mandarin Chinese, he does communicate.

00:06:52.905 --> 00:06:56.715
I don't know, I haven't seen him
discuss philosophy in Chinese, but

00:06:56.715 --> 00:07:04.920
those things that he does talk about he
communicates effectively, uh, I don't

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.800
know, uh, how many tones he gets right.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:11.310
I think it's pretty haphazard,
but he's happy doing it.

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.180
And I think people listening to him
again, I can't speak for Chinese

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:21.315
people, but I have the impression that
Chinese people are happy to see him

00:07:21.375 --> 00:07:24.705
speaking Chinese to the extent that
he speaks it and he is communicating.

00:07:24.885 --> 00:07:31.065
So, uh, I guess where I come to in all of
this is John Cena's Mandarin chinese is

00:07:31.065 --> 00:07:36.705
an example of people who learn a language
for a certain purpose and pull it off

00:07:36.735 --> 00:07:39.195
successfully and obviously enjoy it.

00:07:39.405 --> 00:07:43.215
And really that's what language
learning is all about enjoyment and of

00:07:43.215 --> 00:07:47.145
course, communication or communicating
with people, connecting with people.

00:07:47.235 --> 00:07:51.915
So there you have it, a bit of um,
you know, digression perhaps from

00:07:51.915 --> 00:07:55.635
the title, but just talking about the
importance of communicating without

00:07:55.635 --> 00:07:57.465
worrying too much about perfection.

00:07:58.065 --> 00:07:59.085
Thank you for listening.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:07:59.865
Bye for now.

